
Compiling the Raytracer

To compile the raytracer the following files are required.

* cntlsysT8.occ
* raytraceT8.occ
* framebufT8.occ
* raytraceT8.pgm
* raymainT8.occ

The following steps are required for successful compilation.

imakef raytraceT8.btl        --create the make file
make -f raytraceT8.mak       --run the make file
iserver -sb raytraceT8.btl   --run the raytracer

The imakef statement will create a make file called raytraceT8.mak.
Before executing the make file, the following compiler options must be 
included to allow successful compilation. At the line identified as 
OCCONF= the following compiler options must be appended, as they appear below:

OCCONF=-A -U -NWP -NWU

After these options have been added, save the make file and begin the 
compilation by running the make file.


The raytracer produces an output file called "ray.mtv". To do something useful
with this file (like view it) you need to translate it into something viewable.
On a Sun4, the program "/usr/bin/X11/mtvtoppm" creates a ppm-format file. Use
a program such as "xv" to view this.

	eg. mtvtoppm <ray.mtv | xv


Running it on t4 (2026-11-03)

The shipped raytraceN.btl files run as one threaded network, the model
(1-4) on stdin and the network from the matching map:

	SPYNET=raytrace10.map; export SPYNET
	echo 3 | t4 -st -s8 -sn -10 -sb raytrace10.btl

ray.mtv is the 256 x 256 scene of ../transputer-raytracer/pic/rayM.ppm.
Every arrangement (3, 4, 6, 10, 11, 18, 34) finishes, but some images lack
one 8 x 8 patch (64 pixels), occasionally two: 11 of 28 runs on a buffered
t4, more as the host gets busier.  That is a race in the program, not in
t4.  A worker holds two jobs and passes any further one down the chain;
the last worker's onward channel is "soft", which raytraceT8.pgm loops back
into its own pixelBypass, whose buffer takes the job for an unknown command
and, compiled with -U, skips it.  A job gets that far when the controller
has a finished patch back and sends the next job before the worker that
rendered it has taken its renderer's done ! 42.  On transputers the done
always wins (a few instructions against several link transfers); on t4 a
node is a host thread, and one that the host deschedules in those few
instructions lets the rest of the network overtake it.  Rebuilt without -U
(on t4, with the D7205 tools in ../../itools/d7205), all four checked runs
at 10 nodes stopped on the last worker at the seterr of buffer's IF.
Moving done ! 42 before the pixels is no fix: the controller can then
block feeding a worker whose renderer is still sending, and the farm
deadlocks.  ../transputer-raytracer is the reworked version.

The occam compiler itself (oc) mis-compiled this source on t4 until
7cb262c, which fixed t4's fused eqc;cj pair.
